    "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality When the Exponential is Compared to Zero or a Negative Number",
    "problem": "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {6}^{x+1}+4<1.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {6}^{x+1}+4 & <1 \\\\ 2\\cdot {6}^{x+1} & <-3 \\\\ {6}^{x+1} & <-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $6>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{6}^{x+1}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${6}^{x+1}$ cannot be less than $-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{6}^{x+1}<-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nhas no solutions.",
